/*! @brief The Worker object is used to create child threads and can create and handle child threads in the program. A Worker object can be understood as a JavaScript process running in a thread different from the main thread. Worker does not share memory with the main thread, does not block the main thread, and is a mainstream asynchronous programming method.

The constructor of the Worker object is as follows:

```JavaScript
new Worker(String path, Object opts = {})
```

The path parameter specifies the JavaScript file path of the new thread. For example, you can write a work.js file with the following content:

```JavaScript
const { Worker } = require('worker_threads');
console.log('Hi from worker');
```

In the main program, run work.js with the following code:

```JavaScript
const { Worker } = require('worker_threads');
const worker = new Worker('path/to/work.js');
```

After running, you can see the output "Hi from worker" in the main program's console.

In the following example, suppose we have a long-running calculation that we want to put into another thread for processing while avoiding blocking the main thread. The code is as follows:

Main thread:
```JavaScript
const { Worker } = require('worker_threads');

// create a worker thread
const fib = new Worker(__dirname + '/fib-worker.js');
// Receive results from the worker thread
fib.on('message', (result) => {
  console.log('result: ', result);
});
fib.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error(err);
});
fib.postMessage(40);
console.log('main thread still working');
```

In this example, we create a worker thread through the Worker's constructor to handle Fibonacci sequence calculations. The main thread passes data to the worker thread via the postMessage() method and obtains the processing results through the message event. At the same time, the main thread displays the 'still working' message to prove that this calculation task has been 'delegated' to the worker thread and can continue to handle other things.

The worker thread code style is as follows:

```JavaScript
// fib-worker.js
const { parentPort } = require('worker_threads');

parentPort.on('message', (n) => {
  const result = fib(n);
  // After calculation, the result is sent back to the main thread.
  parentPort.postMessage(result);
});
function fib(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return n;
  return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
}
```

In the worker thread, we listen to the message sent by the main thread through parentPort.on('message'), calculate the specified Fibonacci sequence, and send the calculation result back to the main thread through parentPort.postMessage().

This is a basic Worker example. When developing with Worker objects, the main thread and worker threads are completely asynchronous. Each Worker object is a separate thread, and instantiating a Worker object in the main thread does not cause any blocking.
 */
interface Worker : EventEmitter
{
    /*! @brief Worker object constructor
  @param path specifies the Worker entry script, accepting absolute paths, ./ or ../ relative paths, or source text when opts.eval = true
  @param opts construction options, supports eval and workerData
     */
    Worker(String path, Object opts = {});

  /*! @brief Query the logical worker identifier of the target worker. */
  readonly Integer threadId;

    /*! @brief Send a message to the peer thread,
     @param data specifies the content of the message to send
     */
    postMessage(Value data);

  /*! @brief Terminate the worker. */
  terminate();

    /*! @brief Keep the fibjs process alive. */
    ref();

    /*! @brief Allow the fibjs process to exit. */
    unref();

    /*! @brief Query and bind to receive worker ready event, equivalent to on("online", func); */
    event online();

    /*! @brief Query and bind to receive postMessage message event, equivalent to on("message", func); */
    event message();

    /*! @brief Query and bind to receive error message event, equivalent to on("error", func); */
    event error();

    /*! @brief Query and bind to receive worker exit event, equivalent to on("exit", func); */
    event exit();
};